  "body": "## Summary\n\nThe `setMinLimitOrderAmountInBase()` function lacks validation to ensure the minimum order amount doesn't exceed the lot size, which can create orders that pass minimum size checks but fail lot size validation during amendments, causing perpetual revert loops.\n\n## Finding Description\n\nThis vulnerability exists in the market configuration system where `minLimitOrderAmountInBase` and `lotSizeInBase` are treated as independent parameters without cross-validation. This breaks the system's order lifecycle guarantee by creating impossible-to-amend orders after partial fills.\n\nThe issue manifests through the following sequence:\n1. An operator sets `minLimitOrderAmountInBase` to a value larger than `lotSizeInBase` (e.g., min=100, lot=50)\n2. A large order gets partially filled, leaving a remainder that meets the minimum size requirement but isn't aligned to lot size boundaries\n3. When the system attempts to amend the remaining order, it passes the minimum size check but fails the lot size validation\n4. The amendment operation reverts, leaving the order in an unusable state\n\nThe validation logic in `setMinLimitOrderAmountInBase` only checks against a global minimum but doesn't validate against the current lot size.\n\n## Impact Explanation\n\nThis is a **Medium** severity issue because:\n- It can permanently break the order lifecycle for legitimate large orders\n- Orders become stuck in states where they cannot be amended or completed\n- It affects order book liquidity by preventing proper order management\n- While it doesn't directly cause fund loss, it can lock liquidity in unusable states\n\n## Likelihood Explanation\n\nThe likelihood is **Medium** because:\n- It requires specific configuration where minimum order amount exceeds lot size\n- Market operators may inadvertently create this configuration during routine parameter adjustments\n- The issue only manifests during partial fills of orders, which is a common trading scenario\n- There are no warnings or validations preventing this misconfiguration\n\n## Recommendation\n\nAdd cross-validation between `minLimitOrderAmountInBase` and `lotSizeInBase` in the setter functions:\n\n```solidity\nfunction setMinLimitOrderAmountInBase(Book storage self, uint256 newMinLimitOrderAmountInBase) internal {\n    if (newMinLimitOrderAmountInBase < MIN_MIN_LIMIT_ORDER_AMOUNT_BASE) revert NewMinLimitOrderAmountInvalid();\n    \n    // Add cross-validation with lot size\n    uint256 currentLotSize = self.settings().lotSizeInBase;\n    if (currentLotSize > 0 && newMinLimitOrderAmountInBase > currentLotSize) {\n        revert MinLimitExceedsLotSize();\n    }\n    \n    self.settings().minLimitOrderAmountInBase = newMinLimitOrderAmountInBase;\n    emit MinLimitOrderAmountInBaseUpdated(BookEventNonce.inc(), newMinLimitOrderAmountInBase);\n}\n\nfunction setLotSizeInBase(Book storage self, uint256 newLotSizeInBase) internal {\n    if (newLotSizeInBase == 0) revert NewLotSizeInvalid();\n    \n    // Add cross-validation with minimum order amount\n    uint256 currentMinAmount = self.settings().minLimitOrderAmountInBase;\n    if (newLotSizeInBase < currentMinAmount) {\n        revert LotSizeBelowMinLimit();\n    }\n    \n    self.settings().lotSizeInBase = newLotSizeInBase;\n    emit LotSizeInBaseUpdated(BookEventNonce.inc(), newLotSizeInBase);\n}\n```\n\n## Notes\n\nThe issue stems from treating these parameters independently during configuration but requiring them to work together during order processing. The lot size validation logic and minimum amount validation operate separately without considering their interaction during partial fill scenarios.",
  "proof_of_concept": "Paste in `test/clob/unit/clob/CLOBMinLimitExceedsLotSize.t.sol`\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {CLOBTestBase} from \"test/clob/utils/CLOBTestBase.sol\";\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {BookLib, CLOBStorageLib} from \"contracts/clob/types/Book.sol\";\nimport {Side} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\n\ncontract CLOBMinLimitExceedsLotSizeTest is CLOBTestBase {\n    struct Trace {\n        string fn;\n        bytes params;\n        bool success;\n        bytes data;\n    }\n\n    Trace[] public traces;\n\n    function _pushTrace(string memory fn, bytes memory params, bool success, bytes memory data) internal {\n        traces.push(Trace({fn: fn, params: params, success: success, data: data}));\n    }\n\n    function _traceSetLotSize(uint256 newLotSizeInBase) internal {\n        vm.prank(address(clobManager));\n        (bool success, bytes memory data) = address(clob).call(\n            abi.encodeCall(CLOB.setLotSizeInBase, (newLotSizeInBase))\n        );\n        _pushTrace(\"setLotSizeInBase\", abi.encode(newLotSizeInBase), success, data);\n        require(success, \"set lot size failed\");\n    }\n\n    function _traceSetMinLimit(uint256 newMinLimit) internal {\n        vm.prank(address(clobManager));\n        (bool success, bytes memory data) = address(clob).call(\n            abi.encodeCall(CLOB.setMinLimitOrderAmountInBase, (newMinLimit))\n        );\n        _pushTrace(\"setMinLimitOrderAmountInBase\", abi.encode(newMinLimit), success, data);\n        require(success, \"set min limit failed\");\n    }\n\n    function test_minLimitExceedsLotSize_OrderAmendmentFails() public {\n        uint256 lotSize = 50 ether;\n        uint256 minLimit = 100 ether;\n        _traceSetLotSize(lotSize);\n        _traceSetMinLimit(minLimit);\n\n        address maker = users[0];\n        uint256 price = 100 ether;\n        uint256 amount = 350 ether;\n\n        setupTokens(Side.SELL, maker, amount, price, true);\n\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory pArgs = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amount,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: TOMORROW,\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY\n        });\n\n        vm.prank(maker);\n        (bool success, bytes memory ret) = address(clob).call(\n            abi.encodeCall(CLOB.postLimitOrder, (maker, pArgs))\n        );\n        _pushTrace(\"postLimitOrder\", abi.encode(pArgs), success, ret);\n        require(success, \"post order failed\");\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderResult memory postRes = abi.decode(ret, (ICLOB.PostLimitOrderResult));\n\n        ICLOB.AmendArgs memory aArgs = ICLOB.AmendArgs({\n            orderId: postRes.orderId,\n            amountInBase: 125 ether,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: TOMORROW,\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY\n        });\n\n        vm.prank(maker);\n        (success, ret) = address(clob).call(abi.encodeCall(CLOB.amend, (maker, aArgs)));\n        _pushTrace(\"amend\", abi.encode(aArgs), success, ret);\n\n        // Verify trace entries\n        assertEq(traces.length, 4, \"trace length\");\n        // setMinLimit and setLotSize succeeded\n        assertTrue(traces[0].success, \"lot size call\");\n        assertTrue(traces[1].success, \"min limit call\");\n        // amend should fail with LotSizeInvalid\n        Trace memory t = traces[3];\n        assertEq(t.fn, \"amend\", \"fn name\");\n        assertFalse(t.success, \"amend should revert\");\n        assertEq(t.data, abi.encodeWithSelector(BookLib.LotSizeInvalid.selector), \"error selector\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nRun\n```bash\nforge test --match-test minLimitExceedsLotSize_OrderAmendmentFails -vv\n```\n\nThe `minLimitExceedsLotSize_OrderAmendmentFails` test sets the lot size to 50 ETH and the minimum limit order amount to 100 ETH, proving the contract permits a configuration where the minimum exceeds the lot size. After posting a valid `350 ETH` order, the test attempts to amend that order to 125 ETH—an amount still above the minimum but not aligned with the 50 ETH lot size. The amendment call then reverts with `LotSizeInvalid`, demonstrating that orders passing the minimum-size check can later become unamendable due to lot-size enforcement",
